HTML = f"""<!doctype html>
<html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
<title>Joint Cu–Fe Pourbaix diagram: citrate / glycine / chloride / ammonia electrolyte</title>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=IBM+Plex+Sans:wght@400;600&family=IBM+Plex+Mono&display=swap">
<style>{CSS}</style></head>
<body><main>
<h1>Joint Cu–Fe E–pH predominance diagram</h1>
<p class="lede">1 mM Cu(II) + 1 mM Fe(III) in 10 mM citrate, 10 mM glycine, 0.1 M chloride and 0.1 M total ammonia, 25 °C, I = 0.1 m.
Full mass-balance speciation solved at every point of a 701 × 626 grid (ΔpH = 0.02, ΔE = 4 mV); each cell is coloured by the species that holds the
largest share of the metal, so solid/solution boundaries are 50 %-precipitated lines and aqueous boundaries are equal-concentration lines.
Hydrated solids only: Cu(OH)₂, Cu₂O, Fe(OH)₃(am), Fe(OH)₂ (Fe₂O₃, FeOOH, Fe₃O₄, CuO excluded as requested).</p>

<figure class="fig"><img src="data:image/png;base64,{png}" alt="Three-panel Pourbaix diagram: copper predominance map, iron predominance map, and joint overlay">
<figcaption>(a) Cu and (b) Fe predominance maps; (c) both boundary sets on one frame. Dashed grey lines: H₂/H⁺ and O₂/H₂O at 1 atm; dotted: Cl₂/Cl⁻ at 0.1 M Cl⁻.
Hatched = solid phase. pH is on the concentration scale (p[H]); the activity-scale pH is ≈ 0.11 unit higher at I = 0.1.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>How the two metals partition</h2>
<div class="grid2">
<div class="card"><h3 class="cu">Copper</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Cu(II)</b> (top of the map): free Cu²⁺ only below pH 3.2 (with ≈20 % CuCl⁺); <b>CuCit⁻</b> from pH 3.2 to 5.9; <b>Cu(Gly)₂</b> from pH 5.9 to 11.5 — glycinate beats
the tetra-ammine (Cu(NH₃)₄²⁺ never exceeds ≈1 % even at pH 10–11, because log β₂(Gly) = 15.0 at 10 mM glycinate outweighs log β₄(NH₃) = 11.75 at 0.1 M NH₃); <b>Cu(OH)₂(s)</b> precipitates from pH 11.5 and redissolves as <b>Cu(OH)₄²⁻</b> above pH 13.5.</li>
<li><b>Cu(I)</b> has two separate stability windows: <b>CuCl₂⁻</b> (≈79 % CuCl₂⁻ + 20 % CuCl₃²⁻) between +0.12 and +0.33 V for pH 0–6.0, and <b>Cu(NH₃)₂⁺</b> between ≈0 and −0.20 V for pH 7.0–11.5; in between (pH 6.0–7.0) Cu(I) disproportionates, except for a ≈10 mV <b>Cu₂O(s)</b> sliver (pH 6.2–7.0). Cu₂O is the Cu(I) phase above pH 11.3.</li>
<li><b>Cu(s)</b> deposits below +0.12 V at pH ≤ 5, +0.05 V at pH 7, −0.15 V at pH 9, −0.20 V at pH 11 and −0.31 V at pH 13 — always well above the hydrogen line.</li>
</ul></div>
<div class="card"><h3 class="fe">Iron</h3>
<ul>
<li><b>Fe(III)</b> is citrate-bound above pH 1.2: <b>FeCit(aq)</b> (pH 1.2–2.3), then the bis-citrate family <b>[Fe(Hcit)₂]³⁻</b> (2.3–3.6), <b>[Fe(cit)(Hcit)]⁴⁻</b> (3.6–5.95) and the doubly alkoxide-deprotonated <b>[Fe(cit)₂]⁵⁻</b> (5.95–14). With fresh Fe(OH)₃(am) (log *K<sub>s0</sub> = 4.89) <b>no ferric-hydroxide region exists</b>: 10 mM citrate keeps 1 mM Fe(III) dissolved to pH 14, where Fe(OH)₄⁻ reaches 50 %.</li>
<li><b>Fe(II)</b> is far less stabilised: free <b>Fe²⁺</b> to pH 3.9 (≈5 % FeCl⁺), <b>FeCit⁻</b> pH 3.9–9.1, a narrow <b>Fe(Gly)₂</b> strip (9.1–9.9), then <b>Fe(OH)₂(s)</b> above pH 9.9.</li>
<li>Because citrate binds Fe(III) ~10¹⁰–10²⁵ times more strongly than Fe(II), the Fe(III)/Fe(II) line falls from +0.74 V (pH &lt; 1.2) to +0.17 V at pH 3.6, −0.16 V at pH 6, −0.55 V at pH 9 and stays at ≈ −0.59 V ([Fe(cit)₂]⁵⁻/Fe(OH)₂) for pH 10–14. The Fe(II) window is therefore only 0.03–0.2 V wide above pH 6.</li>
<li><b>Fe(s)</b> needs −0.56 V (pH ≤ 4) to −0.90 V (pH 14): 0.1–0.4 V below the hydrogen line everywhere.</li>
</ul></div>
</div>

<h3>Equilibrium deposition potentials (50 % lines) and their separation</h3>
{table(['pH', 'Cu(s) top edge / V', 'Fe(s) top edge / V', 'ΔE(Cu−Fe) / V', 'H₂ line (1 atm) / V', 'Fe line − H₂ line / V'], dep_rows)}
<p>The Cu–Fe separation is smallest (≈0.48 V) around pH 9–10, where Cu(II) is held as Cu(Gly)₂ and Cu(I) as Cu(NH₃)₂⁺ while Fe(II) is only weakly complexed; it is ≈0.68 V in acid. Fe deposition is never thermodynamically separable from hydrogen evolution in this bath.</p>

<h2>Sub-speciation within each oxidation state (E-independent)</h2>
<p>Species holding ≥ 2 % of the metal when that oxidation state carries all of it; the last column lists the free-ligand concentrations the solver found (log₁₀ mol/L), which are what enter the boundary equations below.</p>
<h3 class="cu">Cu(II)</h3>{table(['pH', 'species (% of Cu)', 'log free ligand'], sub_rows('Cu(2)'))}
<h3 class="cu">Cu(I)</h3>{table(['pH', 'species (% of Cu)', 'log free ligand'], sub_rows('Cu(1)'))}
<h3 class="fe">Fe(III)</h3>{table(['pH', 'species (% of Fe)', 'log free ligand'], sub_rows('Fe(3)'))}
<h3 class="fe">Fe(II)</h3>{table(['pH', 'species (% of Fe)', 'log free ligand'], sub_rows('Fe(2)'))}

<h2>Predominance regions</h2>
<h3 class="cu">Copper — 9 regions</h3>{table(['species', 'state', 'phase', 'pH extent', 'E extent / V', 'share of map'], region_rows('Cu'))}
<h3 class="fe">Iron — 11 regions</h3>{table(['species', 'state', 'phase', 'pH extent', 'E extent / V', 'share of map'], region_rows('Fe'))}

<h2>Region boundaries</h2>
<p>Every boundary of the diagram, with the closed-form line implied by the constants the solver used. Ligand terms use the solver's free-ligand concentrations
(table above), which is why redox lines through the citrate/glycine range are curved; sample points are read off the numerical boundary.
For solid/aqueous pairs the line is drawn where the aqueous species reaches 0.5 mM in equilibrium with the solid; minor co-species shift the numerical line by a few mV.</p>
<h3 class="cu">Copper</h3>{table(['boundary (A | B)', 'type', 'extent', 'equation (E in V vs SHE, log[L] = free ligand, mol/L)', 'numerical points (pH, E)'], boundary_rows('Cu'))}
<h3 class="fe">Iron</h3>{table(['boundary (A | B)', 'type', 'extent', 'equation (E in V vs SHE, log[L] = free ligand, mol/L)', 'numerical points (pH, E)'], boundary_rows('Fe'))}

<h2>Junctions (triple points)</h2>
<p>Located where three predominance regions meet on the grid (±0.02 pH, ±4 mV).</p>
<h3 class="cu">Copper</h3>{table(['regions meeting', 'pH', 'E / V', 'remark'], junction_rows('Cu'))}
<h3 class="fe">Iron</h3>{table(['regions meeting', 'pH', 'E / V', 'remark'], junction_rows('Fe'))}

<h2>Equilibrium constants and potentials used by the solver</h2>
<p>Formation constants from the components Cu²⁺, Fe³⁺, Cit³⁻ (ordinary citrate trianion), Gly⁻, Cl⁻, NH₃, H⁺ and e⁻ (pe = E/0.05916).
"log K used" is the concentration constant at I = 0.1 m that entered the calculation. I = 0 data were converted with the Davies equation
(log γ = −0.1071 z² at I = 0.1 m); potentials were converted with log K = nE°/0.05916 V and then Davies-corrected, i.e. E°′(Cu²⁺/Cu) = 0.327 V,
E°′(Cu⁺/Cu) = 0.515 V, E°′(Cu²⁺/Cu⁺) = 0.140 V, E°′(Fe³⁺/Fe²⁺) = 0.739 V, E°′(Fe²⁺/Fe) = −0.460 V on this scale.</p>
{table(['group', 'species', 'formation reaction', 'log K used', 'basis', 'source / note'], species_rows())}

<h2>Assumptions, sensitivity and caveats</h2>
<div class="note">
<p><b>Ferric citrate model.</b> The result that Fe(OH)₃ never precipitates rests on the bis-citrate family with alkoxide deprotonation
(Vukosav, Mlakar &amp; Tomišić; converted to the Cit³⁻ basis with the citrate alcohol pKa = 14.4 of Silva, Kong &amp; Hider) together with a <em>fresh</em> hydrous ferric oxide.
Sensitivity run with an aged hydroxide (log *K<sub>s0</sub> = 3.2 instead of 4.89): {sens_txt}. Older 1:1 models (FeCitH₋₁⁻, log β ≈ 9) would bring Fe(OH)₃ in around pH 8–9.
